<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="using:Hoard.App.ViewModels"
             x:Class="Hoard.App.Views.FeedCardView"
             x:DataType="vm:FeedCardViewModel">

    <!--
        ══ A feed card ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        The cover, the name, and THE SENTENCE — in full, on the front, without
        hovering, without trimming.

        THE CARD IS SIZED TO THE SENTENCE AND NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND. Measured
        on the real library (46 items across five shelves): the reasons run 78
        to 256 characters, median 115, 90th percentile 155. A card that trimmed
        the reason to fit would be a cover in a grid, which is the storefront
        feed this app exists to beat.

        ══ WHAT THE GRID CHANGED, AND IT IS THE MEASURE ═════════════════════
        This card was 344px wide with a 120x180 cover, and both numbers were
        the rail's. A fixed width is what a horizontally scrolling row needs;
        with the sections now wrapping grids (FeedView.axaml) the card takes
        whatever width its column got, and FeedGrid holds a floor of 420 so the
        column it is given is always at least that.

        The shape did NOT change — cover left, sentence right is still correct,
        because the sentence is the payload and putting it beside the art is
        what keeps it the payload. The PROPORTIONS did. At 344 the text column
        was 182px, which is about twenty-seven characters: a newspaper column,
        well under the readable band, and six lines deep at the 90th
        percentile. At the 443px a card gets at the 1200px minimum width, the
        same column is 291px and about forty-four characters — four lines for
        the same sentence, and a shorter card for it.

        So the cover shrinks with it, 120x180 -> 108x162. That is TileMinWidth
        exactly: the smallest a capsule is ever drawn on the wall, and still
        large enough to recognise. It is what stops the art, rather than the
        prose, from setting the card's height — which on a surface whose whole
        job is the reason would be the geometry arguing with the point.

        WHY THIS CARD DOES NOT FLIP. The library's tiles turn over because a
        cover has nowhere to put four facts. This face is already showing the
        one fact that matters, and every fact on the tile's back — the bucket,
        the playtime, the last-played year — is a clause of the sentence printed
        here. Turning it over would hide the reason to reveal a weaker
        restatement of it. Both actions the back face carries are on this face
        instead: the primary Play/Install, and the detail modal, which the whole
        card opens.

        THE CARD IS A BUTTON, and that is what buys most of §8's floor for free:
        a Tab stop, Enter and Space, and a press that opens the detail modal. The
        Play button inside it marks its own press handled, so the two never both
        fire. What a Button does NOT give is a ring anyone can see — Fluent
        repaints its own border by a shade and calls that focus — so the 2px Volt
        one is drawn below, and it was drawn only after looking at the running
        app and failing to find the framework's.

        FLARE APPEARS HERE EXACTLY ONCE, on the unread dot, and only on a cover
        whose game has been patched since it was played. It is not on the shelf
        heading, not on the card edge, and not on the patched shelf's cards as a
        set — the badge means something for as long as nothing else wears that
        colour (§2).
    -->

    <UserControl.Styles>

        <!-- The card. Opaque Surface over the pane's own (possibly translucent)
             ground, on the same rule the Stores cards follow: anything carrying
             reading matter or cover art is a solid field. It is also what the
             dormancy cross-fade needs — the two art layers are only opaque
             together once both have decoded, and a partly transparent tile shows
             whatever the window is standing on. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource Surface}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource LineSoft}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusTile}"/>
            <!-- Zero, with the inset moved onto the content below: the focus
                 ring has to be drawn at the CARD's edge rather than at its
                 content's, and a ring inside the padding would be a rectangle
                 floating 14px in from the border it is supposed to be marking. -->
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- Elevation is the Surface → SurfaceRaised step (§4). The one place a
             drop shadow is permitted is a tile on the wall, and a card is not
             one — what lifts under the pointer here is the ART, by waking up. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource Surface}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource LineSoft}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusTile}"/>
            <Setter Property="Transitions">
                <Transitions>
                    <BrushTransition Property="Background" Duration="0:0:0.14"/>
                </Transitions>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceRaised}"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHigh}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ══ §8's focus ring: 2px Volt, visible, and it does not reflow ═══
             Drawn as an element inside the card rather than as a border-brush
             swap on the presenter, for the reason the wall's tiles draw their
             selection edge the same way: §10.7 forbids a thickness change on
             focus (it moves everything the border encloses), and a 1px swap
             cannot be the 2px §8 asks for. An element can be exactly 2px and
             costs the layout nothing, because it is not in the flow.

             The presenter's own border is PINNED on focus so the Fluent theme
             does not repaint it underneath this: two focus indicators on one
             control is how the weaker one ends up being the one people learn. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:focus-visible /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource LineSoft}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Border#FocusRing">
            <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:focus-visible Border#FocusRing">
            <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- §5.1's dormancy cross-fade, and the 140ms restore that is the most
             important interaction in the app: the game wakes up under the
             cursor. The transition lives in a style rather than on the element
             because §8's reduced-motion setting has to be able to take it away,
             and a local Transitions value would outrank any selector trying to. -->
        <Style Selector="Panel#Art Border#VividArt, Panel#Art Image#VividArtwork">
            <Setter Property="Transitions">
                <Transitions>
                    <DoubleTransition Property="Opacity" Duration="0:0:0.14" Easing="CubicEaseOut"/>
                </Transitions>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <!-- Reduced motion: the state snaps instead of fading (§8). An empty
             transition set, not a zero duration — there is then no animation to
             schedule at all. -->
        <Style Selector="Panel#Art.snap Border#VividArt, Panel#Art.snap Image#VividArtwork">
            <Setter Property="Transitions">
                <Transitions/>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- Compact action. The app's one primary treatment (Volt fill) at the
             card's scale; the sizes are local values because nothing overrides
             them and a second class for two numbers would be furniture. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.cardact">
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,6"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- ══ The two feedback controls ═══════════════════════════════════
             "Not interested" and "Not now", and everything about this style is
             about WEIGHT. The reason sentence is the card's payload; these are
             the reply to it, and a reply that competes with what it is replying
             to has taken the screen over.

             So: no fill, no border, TextDim, 11px, beside a Volt-filled Play at
             12px with a solid ground under it. Three axes of difference in the
             same line, and the eye sorts them in one pass.

             THEY ARE ALWAYS VISIBLE, and that is a decision rather than a
             default. Revealing them on hover would have bought a quieter resting
             card and cost them to everyone navigating by keyboard and everyone
             on a touch screen — a hover-only control is one that some people
             simply do not have. What hover and focus buy instead is CONTRAST:
             the pair lifts from TextDim to Text when the pointer or the keyboard
             is on the card. At rest TextDim on Surface measures 5.88:1, well
             over §8's floor, so quiet here never means unreadable.

             They cost the sentence NO HEIGHT: they sit in the action line the
             card already had, whose height is set by the Play button beside
             them. The reason above is untouched, un-trimmed and un-tooltipped,
             exactly as it was.

             BorderThickness is a constant 2 with the resting brush transparent,
             so §8's focus ring is a brush swap and never a reflow (§10.7) — a
             control that shifts sideways as you tab onto it is worse than no
             ring. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.cardaside">
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource BodyFont}"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2"/>
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextDim}"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.cardaside /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- The card is hovered or the keyboard is inside it: the pair comes up
             to full ink. This is the only thing hover buys them — never their
             existence. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:pointerover Button.cardaside /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard:focus-within Button.cardaside /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- Its own hover is a raised field rather than a colour: a dismiss
             control is not an alarm, and Flare is the badge's alone (§2). -->
        <Style Selector="Button.cardaside:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHigh}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.cardaside:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHigh}"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.cardaside:focus-visible /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Volt}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A card the user has answered. Only the ART dims — the title and the
             sentence stay at full ink, because "the reason stays fully visible
             on the front" does not stop being true the moment somebody replies
             to it, and a dimmed paragraph is a paragraph somebody has to squint
             at to remember what they just did. The art is the one element on the
             card that is not reading matter, so it is the one that can carry
             "this is out of the way now" for nothing.

             The state is said in words as well, on the receipt line — §8's
             decorative-redundancy rule, the same one the dormancy ramp lives
             under. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.feedcard.setaside Panel#Art">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.42"/>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Styles>

    <Button Name="Card"
            Classes="feedcard"
            Classes.setaside="{Binding IsSetAside}"
            Command="{Binding Tile.OpenDetailsCommand}"
            CommandParameter="{Binding Tile}">
        <!-- No tooltip on the card, and that is a correction made at the glass
             rather than a preference. Hovering a card is the RESTING state of
             reading this screen, so a tooltip on it pops up over the sentence on
             the card beside it — the interface covering the one thing it exists
             to show. The pointer already turns, the card already lifts to
             SurfaceRaised, and the art already wakes up; that is three signals
             that it is a target, none of which is in the way. -->
        <Panel>
        <Grid Margin="14" ColumnDefinitions="108,14,*">

            <!-- ══ The art ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
                 The same two-layer cross-fade the wall uses (the floor variant
                 under the vivid one, whose opacity carries the ramp), the same
                 placeholder underneath it, and the same unread badge on top.
                 Reproduced rather than shared because a tile is a 2:3 cell with
                 a hover scrim and a back face, and this is a 2:3 illustration
                 beside a paragraph — the two would fight over what the pointer
                 means. What they DO share is the view model, so a cover, a ramp
                 position and a badge can never differ between the two screens. -->
            <Panel Grid.Column="0"
                   Name="Art"
                   Width="108" Height="162"
                   VerticalAlignment="Top"
                   Classes.snap="{Binding Tile.SnapDormancy}">

                <Border CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusTile}" ClipToBounds="True"
                        BorderThickness="1" BorderBrush="{StaticResource LineSoft}">
                    <Panel>
                        <!-- Opaque ground under both art layers: between the
                             first decoded bitmap and the second, a dimmed cover
                             is a partly transparent one. -->
                        <Border Background="{StaticResource TileGround}"/>

                        <!-- §7's placeholder: the title in Bricolage on a
                             Surface field, until real art lands and whenever a
                             game has none. Never a spinner, never a hole. -->
                        <Border Background="{Binding Tile.FloorBrush}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding Tile.ShowPlaceholder}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Tile.Title}"
                                       FontFamily="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"
                                       FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="15" LineHeight="17"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                       Foreground="{Binding Tile.FloorTitleBrush}"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="10"/>
                        </Border>
                        <Border Name="VividArt"
                                Background="{Binding Tile.VividBrush}"
                                Opacity="{Binding Tile.DisplayAlpha}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding Tile.ShowPlaceholder}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Tile.Title}"
                                       FontFamily="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"
                                       FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="15" LineHeight="17"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                       Foreground="White"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="10"/>
                        </Border>

                        <Image Source="{Binding Tile.FloorCover}" Stretch="UniformToFill"/>
                        <Image Name="VividArtwork"
                               Source="{Binding Tile.VividCover}" Stretch="UniformToFill"
                               Opacity="{Binding Tile.DisplayAlpha}"/>

                        <!-- Gloss sweep, as on the wall. -->
                        <Border IsHitTestVisible="False">
                            <Border.Background>
                                <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="70%,100%">
                                    <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#29FFFFFF"/>
                                    <GradientStop Offset="0.42" Color="#00FFFFFF"/>
                                </LinearGradientBrush>
                            </Border.Background>
                        </Border>
                    </Panel>
                </Border>

                <!-- The unread badge (§5.2), outside the clip so its glow is not
                     sheared off at the corner. Flare's one use, here as on the
                     wall: an update landed after the last session. Never on a
                     never-opened game — an unplayed game has nothing to be
                     behind on — and the sentence beside it says the same thing
                     in words, which is §8's redundancy. -->
                <Border Width="14" Height="14" CornerRadius="7"
                        Background="{StaticResource Flare}"
                        BorderBrush="{StaticResource Ground}" BorderThickness="2"
                        BoxShadow="{DynamicResource BadgeGlow}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Margin="0,6,6,0"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Tile.HasUnread}"/>
            </Panel>

            <!-- ══ The name, the sentence, the way in ═══════════════════════ -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Column="2" LastChildFill="True">

                <!-- ══ The action line ═════════════════════════
                     Actions sit at the bottom of the card whatever the sentence
                     does above them, so a row of cards has one action line
                     rather than a ragged one.

                     ══ AND IT IS WHERE THE FEEDBACK CONTROLS GO ════════════
                     "Not interested" and "not now" had one hard requirement:
                     they must not compete with the reason, crowd it, or push it
                     below the fold. Putting them HERE is the whole answer, and
                     it is a geometry answer rather than a styling one — this
                     line already exists and its height is already set by the
                     Play button, so two more controls on it cost the sentence
                     exactly nothing. Anywhere else on the card — a row of their
                     own, a strip under the reason, a corner over the art — would
                     have been paid for in the one currency this screen has none
                     of.

                     They sit a Play button away from Play and three weights
                     below it (no fill, no border, TextDim, 11px against a
                     Volt-filled 12px), so the primary action still reads first
                     in the line. See the styles above for why they are always
                     visible rather than revealed on hover.

                     ══ AND THE UNDO IS THIS SAME LINE ═══════════════════
                     Acting swaps the row for a one-line receipt with Undo in it.
                     The card does not vanish, does not shrink and does not move
                     the ones beside it: the row's height is unchanged, so
                     nothing in the grid reflows under a user who has just
                     clicked. The undo therefore appears within a few pixels of
                     the control that was pressed, which is the only place a
                     misclick can be caught without hunting — and it stays until
                     the feed is next computed rather than counting down. -->
                <Panel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Margin="0,10,0,0">

                    <!-- The unanswered state. -->
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto"
                          IsVisible="{Binding ShowActions}">

                        <!-- Play when it is on disk, Install when it is not, and
                             NOTHING when the app cannot honestly name either — no
                             id for the store, no verified install route, or no
                             answer at all about the install state (§10.3: never
                             an inert button). The same command the wall's tiles
                             raise, so a launch from here is attributed exactly
                             like any other. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="0"
                                Classes="act primary cardact"
                                Margin="0,0,8,0"
                                Content="{Binding Tile.PrimaryActionLabel}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Tile.PrimaryActionHint}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding Tile.HasPrimaryAction}"
                                Command="{Binding Tile.PrimaryActionCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding Tile}"/>

                        <!-- The durable verdict. Not a destructive act and not
                             styled as one: Danger is the window's close button
                             and nothing else (§2), and Flare belongs to the
                             unread dot two inches to the left. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="1"
                                Classes="cardaside"
                                Content="Not interested"
                                IsVisible="{Binding CanGiveFeedback}"
                                Command="{Binding NotInterestedCommand}"/>

                        <!-- The deferral. A separate control because it is a
                             separate intent — the storage keeps the two apart
                             (§6b) and one button that guessed between them would
                             be where the difference was lost. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="2"
                                Classes="cardaside"
                                Content="Not now"
                                IsVisible="{Binding CanGiveFeedback}"
                                Command="{Binding NotNowCommand}"/>

                        <!-- The one fact the sentence never states. A name, so it
                             wears the outlined badge the tile's overlay uses
                             rather than a colour. -->
                        <Border Grid.Column="4"
                                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Line}"
                                BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="3" Padding="5,1"
                                Margin="8,0,0,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Tile.StoreBadge}"
                                       FontFamily="{StaticResource BodyFont}"
                                       FontSize="9" LetterSpacing="0.54"
                                       Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"/>
                        </Border>
                    </Grid>

                    <!-- The receipt. Same line, same height, same place — what
                         changed is what it says. The prose and the date are two
                         runs so the date sets in Plex Mono with tabular figures
                         (§3) while the words beside it stay in the body face,
                         which is the rule the reason sentence above follows. -->
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto"
                          IsVisible="{Binding IsSetAside}">
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="0"
                                    Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                       Text="{Binding SetAsideNote}"/>
                            <TextBlock Classes="data"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding HasSetAsideDate}"
                                       Text="{Binding SetAsideDate}"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <!-- Quiet rather than primary: undoing is the way back,
                             not the thing to do next. It is a real bordered
                             control though, where the two that put the card here
                             are borderless — after the act, the way back is the
                             only offer on the line and can afford to be found
                             instantly. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="1"
                                Classes="act quiet cardact"
                                Content="Undo"
                                Command="{Binding UndoCommand}"/>
                    </Grid>
                </Panel>

                <!-- A write that did not land. Both controls stay exactly where
                     they were so the user can simply press again, and the card
                     grows by one line rather than claiming a dismissal the
                     database does not hold. Amber, because §2 gives Amber
                     attention and Danger the one destructive act — a failed save
                     is neither an alarm nor a thing to undo. -->
                <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom"
                           Classes="body"
                           FontSize="11"
                           Margin="0,8,0,0"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource Amber}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasProblem}"
                           Text="{Binding Problem}"/>

                <StackPanel Spacing="6">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Tile.Title}"
                               FontFamily="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"
                               FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="15" LineHeight="17"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxLines="2"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource Text}"/>

                    <!-- THE REASON. No MaxLines, no TextTrimming, no tooltip —
                         it wraps to whatever it needs and the card grows. Its
                         inlines are built in the code-behind so every number in
                         it sets in Plex Mono with tabular figures (§3) while the
                         sentence keeps the body face: the feed is where this
                         app's numbers moved out of columns and into prose, and
                         they are still numbers. FontFeatures is set here and
                         inherited by those runs. -->
                    <TextBlock Name="Reason"
                               Classes="body"
                               FontFeatures="tnum"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               LineHeight="18"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource Text}"/>
                </StackPanel>

            </DockPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- Drawn INSIDE the card's own bounds, exactly as the tile's selection
             edge is and for the same reason: §8 asks for a 2px ring at a 2px
             offset, and an outward offset on a card at the end of a rail is a
             ring the ScrollViewer clips — a half-drawn ring reads as a
             rendering fault rather than as focus. The 1px Ground separator
             inside it is the tile's too, so the ring reads as a ring and not as
             a thicker border. -->
        <Border Name="FocusRing"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Volt}" BorderThickness="2"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusTile}"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource Ground}" BorderThickness="1"
                    CornerRadius="4"/>
        </Border>
        </Panel>
    </Button>

</UserControl>
